# Bybit v5 REST responses, written from the documented formats. Not captured
# from a live session; numbers are made up to fit the tests. `retMsg` is the
# documented wording, shortened; the adapter reads `retCode` only. The three
# `X-Bapi-Limit*` headers are shown where the docs show them: on the
# responses that report the endpoint's quota, not on every success. Requests
# went out at server time 1700000000000 ms.
# - Rate limits, usage headers, the IP ban: https://bybit-exchange.github.io/docs/v5/rate-limit
# - Error and HTTP codes: https://bybit-exchange.github.io/docs/v5/error
# - Place order response: https://bybit-exchange.github.io/docs/v5/order/create-order

### order-ok
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
X-Bapi-Limit: 10
X-Bapi-Limit-Status: 9

{"retCode":0,"retMsg":"OK","result":{"orderId":"1321003749386327552","orderLinkId":"spot-test-postonly"},"retExtInfo":{},"time":1700000000000}

### too-many-visits
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
X-Bapi-Limit: 10
X-Bapi-Limit-Status: 0
X-Bapi-Limit-Reset-Timestamp: 1700000000400

{"retCode":10006,"retMsg":"Too many visits","result":{},"retExtInfo":{},"time":1700000000000}

### ip-limit-10018
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{"retCode":10018,"retMsg":"Exceeded the IP Rate Limit.","result":{},"retExtInfo":{},"time":1700000000000}

### ip-ban-403
HTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden
Content-Type: text/plain

access too frequent

### forbidden-403
# 403 has other causes too (a GET with an empty JSON body, a U.S. IP).
# Halted anyway: the safe side, until a person clears it.
HTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden
Content-Type: text/plain

Forbidden

### protection-429
# "System level frequency protection": not this account's quota.
HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
Content-Length: 0


